    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Which Islands Can You See From Madeira, and When?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>14 December 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">From Madeira you can see the Desertas, three long, bare islands about 30 to 40 km south-east of Funchal, on most clear days, and Porto Santo, roughly 40 km north-east, only from high or eastern points such as Pico do Arieiro and Ponta de São Lourenço. The Selvagens, about 280 km south, are never visible.</p>

      <h2>Which islands are on the horizon, and how far away are they?</h2>
<p>Two groups can be seen from Madeira and one never can. This is the short version:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Island</th><th>Where it lies</th><th>Where to see it from</th><th>How often</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Desertas (Ilhéu Chão, Deserta Grande, Bugio)</td><td>About 30 to 40 km south-east of Funchal. Deserta Grande is about 25 km from Ponta de São Lourenço.</td><td>Funchal's seafront and viewpoints, the sea off the south coast, Ponta de São Lourenço</td><td>Most clear days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Porto Santo</td><td>Roughly 40 km north-east of Madeira's eastern tip</td><td>Pico do Arieiro, Ponta de São Lourenço</td><td>Clear days only, from high or eastern points</td></tr>
<tr><td>Selvagens</td><td>About 280 km south</td><td>Nowhere on Madeira</td><td>Never</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The Canary Islands are further still, beyond the Selvagens. Whatever you may hear in a bar, you will not see Tenerife from Madeira.</p>
<h2>What do the Desertas look like from Funchal?</h2>
<p>Like a long, low wall on the sea to the south-east. The chain runs about 22 km from north to south, so it fills a wide stretch of the horizon. On a clear day you can tell the three islands apart.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ilhéu Chão</strong>, the northern one, is flat and low, about 100 metres high. It looks like a table.</li>
<li><strong>Deserta Grande</strong>, in the middle, is the long one, with cliffs rising to over 400 metres.</li>
<li><strong>Bugio</strong>, the southern end, is narrow and about 350 metres high, often the faintest of the three.</li>
</ul>
<p>Visit Madeira names the Pico dos Barcelos viewpoint, at 355 metres above Funchal, and the Neves viewpoint as good places to see them on land. From sea level they look lower but closer, because nothing stands in the way.</p>
<p>The Desertas are a nature reserve and the home of Madeira's monk seals. Our <a href="/posts/desertas-islands-madeira-guide">guide to the Desertas Islands</a> covers their history and how to visit them.</p>
<h2>Can you see Porto Santo from Funchal?</h2>
<p>Usually not. Porto Santo lies north-east of Madeira, and from Funchal and the south-west coast the eastern end of Madeira itself stands in the line of sight. To see it you need height or a position further east.</p>
<p>Visit Madeira says Porto Santo shows from Pico do Arieiro, at 1,818 metres, on clear days. Walkers also see it from the end of the Ponta de São Lourenço trail when the air is sharp. The view also works the other way: from the Ponta da Canavieira viewpoint on Porto Santo, the northern shore of Madeira appears when visibility is good.</p>
<p>If you want to go there rather than look at it, the ferry from Funchal takes about two and a half hours. We explain the options in <a href="/posts/can-you-go-to-porto-santo-by-boat-from-madeira">can you go to Porto Santo by boat</a>.</p>
<h2>Why will you never see the Selvagens?</h2>
<p>They are too far and too low. The Selvagens are part of the Madeira region, but they lie about 280 km to the south, roughly halfway to the Canaries. At that distance even a tall island would sit well below the curve of the earth seen from Madeira's highest peak, and the Selvagens are small and flat.</p>
<p>They matter to anyone on a boat here anyway. The biggest colony of Cory's shearwaters in the world breeds on the Selvagens, and some of the large brown and white birds you see gliding past the boat off Funchal belong to the same species.</p>
<h2>What weather gives the clearest view?</h2>
<p>Clean, dry air, more than blue sky. A sunny day can still hide every island behind a pale haze. Two things decide it:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Haze.</strong> When dust from the Sahara blows over, a day locals call calima, the horizon turns milky and the Desertas can vanish completely, even with the sun out. Read more in <a href="/posts/calima-in-madeira-and-boat-trips">what calima does to a boat trip</a>.</li>
<li><strong>The air after rain.</strong> The sharpest views often come on the first fine day after a spell of rain, when showers have washed the air. In winter that can mean the Desertas look close enough to swim to.</li>
</ol>
<p>The time of day changes the picture too. In the morning the sun is on the same side of the sky as the Desertas, seen from Funchal, so they often show as dark shapes against the glare. In the late afternoon the sun is behind you, low in the west, and it lights their western cliffs. That is when they look most solid.</p>
<h2>Where do you see them from on a boat trip?</h2>
<p>Look back. Both Chifbay trips leave Marina do Funchal and turn west, so the Desertas are behind you, off the stern, for most of the way out. Once the harbour wall is behind you, turn round: on a clear day the whole chain sits on the horizon beyond the city.</p>
<p>The return is better still. On the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a>, the boat turns at Cabo Girão or Ribeira Brava and heads back east in the last of the light, with the Desertas ahead of you. On some evenings the sky above them turns pink and then grey long after the sun has gone behind you.</p>
<p>On the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> the same thing happens in brighter light on the fast run home. Neither trip goes to the Desertas: they are a view on our trips, not a stop. Tour boats that do cross to them need about one and a half to two hours each way, depending on the boat and the sea.</p>
<p>Honest note: some days you will see nothing at all on the horizon. Islands at 30 or 40 km are at the mercy of the air, and no skipper can change that.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Which islands are visible from Funchal?</h3><p>The Desertas. On a clear day the chain of Ilhéu Chão, Deserta Grande and Bugio sits on the south-eastern horizon.</p><h3>Can you see Porto Santo from Pico do Arieiro?</h3><p>Yes, on clear days. Visit Madeira lists it among the views from the 1,818 metre summit.</p><h3>How far are the Desertas from Madeira?</h3><p>About 30 to 40 km south-east of Funchal, and about 25 km from Ponta de São Lourenço, Madeira's eastern tip.</p><h3>Do Chifbay trips go to the Desertas?</h3><p>No. Both trips run west along the south coast. The Desertas are part of the view, mostly on the way back.</p>

      <blockquote>The Desertas are always there. Whether you see them is up to the air, not the calendar.</blockquote>
    </article>
